      \u003cgmd:LocalisedCharacterString locale\u003d\"#EN\"\u003eMarine Aggreggates Levy Sustainability Fund (MALSF) Outer Bristol Channel Marine Habitat Study (Biomor 4 report) - Multibeam Data (3rd Party Data)\u003c/gmd:LocalisedCharacterString\u003e               \u003c/gmd:textGroup\u003e             \u003c/gmd:PT_FreeText\u003e           \u003c/gmd:title\u003e           \u003cgmd:alternateTitle\u003e             \u003cgco:CharacterString\u003eAstudiaeth o Gynefin Morol Mor Hafren Allanol - data aml-donfedd\u003c/gco:CharacterString\u003e           \u003c/gmd:alternateTitle\u003e           \u003cgmd:date\u003e             \u003cgmd:CI_Date\u003e               \u003cgmd:date\u003e                 \u003cgco:Date\u003e2006-11-30\u003c/gco:Date\u003e               \u003c/gmd:date\u003e               \u003cgmd:dateType\u003e                 \u003cgmd:CI_DateTypeCode codeList\u003d\"http://standards.iso.org/iso/19139/resources/gmxCodelists.xml#CI_DateTypeCode\" codeListValue\u003d\"publication\" /\u003e               \u003c/gmd:dateType\u003e             \u003c/gmd:CI_Date\u003e           \u003c/gmd:date\u003e           \u003cgmd:identifier\u003e             \u003cgmd:MD_Identifier\u003e               \u003cgmd:code\u003e                 \u003cgco:CharacterString\u003eEXT_DS114487\u003c/gco:CharacterString\u003e               \u003c/gmd:code\u003e             \u003c/gmd:MD_Identifier\u003e           \u003c/gmd:identifier\u003e         \u003c/gmd:CI_Citation\u003e       \u003c/gmd:citation\u003e       \u003cgmd:abstract xsi:type\u003d\"gmd:PT_FreeText_PropertyType\"\u003e         \u003cgco:CharacterString\u003eThe Outer Bristol Channel Marine Habitat Study was a multifaceted project involving scientific investigation Interpretation and education in an area with potential marine aggregate resources. The Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary boarded by Wales and England, has the largest tidal range in UK waters and is a multiple-use environment. Natural resource activities include aggregate extraction, fisheries and wind-farm developments. Marine nature conservation is prominent with Lundy Marine Nature Reserve in the south and, under the EC Habitats Directive, a Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this study was to undertake a variety of work to collect and provide data and interpretations on the current physical state of the sea bed environment in terms of biology, sediment, geology and morphology. The study was jointly lead by the British Geological Survey (BGS) and Amgueddfa Cymru - National Museum Wales (NMW) and supported by core funding from the Aggregate Levy Sustainable Fund (ALSF) in Wales and England. Additional support came from The Crown Estate and the British Marine Aggregate Producers Association (BMAPA). There were numerous aims to this project, as outlined in the report (see related titles). This dataset just covers one specific aim: To undertake a marine geophysical survey utilising multibeam, sidescan sonar and sub-bottom seismic reflection systems. This dataset comprises of multibeam bathymetry data for the Outer Bristol Channel, including raw data files, cruise logs, calibration data, processed data and geotiffs. As well as the sidescan sonar and sub bottom profile data.  The purpose of this data capture was to collect multibeam data for the Biomor project to give a 3D image of the seabed, and enable display of sandbanks, ascertain slope steepness and identify other seabed features. Additional backscatter and acoustic data along with benthic sampling enabled the multibeam data to be used to classify and map the seabed.\u003c/gco:CharacterString\u003e         \u003cgmd:PT_FreeText\u003e           \u003cgmd:textGroup\u003e             \u003cgmd:LocalisedCharacterString locale\u003d\"#EN\"\u003eThe Outer Bristol Channel Marine Habitat Study was a multifaceted project involving scientific investigation Interpretation and education in an area with potential marine aggregate resources. The Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary boarded by Wales and England, has the largest tidal range in UK waters and is a multiple-use environment. Natural resource activities include aggregate extraction, fisheries and wind-farm developments. Marine nature conservation is prominent with Lundy Marine Nature Reserve in the south and, under the EC Habitats Directive, a Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this study was to undertake a variety of work to collect and provide data and interpretations on the current physical state of the sea bed environment in terms of biology, sediment, geology and morphology. The study was jointly lead by the British Geological Survey (BGS) and Amgueddfa Cymru - National Museum Wales (NMW) and supported by core funding from the Aggregate Levy Sustainable Fund (ALSF) in Wales and England. Additional support came from The Crown Estate and the British Marine Aggregate Producers Association (BMAPA). There were numerous aims to this project, as outlined in the report (see related titles). This dataset just covers one specific aim: To undertake a marine geophysical survey utilising multibeam, sidescan sonar and sub-bottom seismic reflection systems. This dataset comprises of multibeam bathymetry data for the Outer Bristol Channel, including raw data files, cruise logs, calibration data, processed data and geotiffs. As well as the sidescan sonar and sub bottom profile data.  The purpose of this data capture was to collect multibeam data for the Biomor project to give a 3D image of the seabed, and enable display of sandbanks, ascertain slope steepness and identify other seabed features. Additional backscatter and acoustic data along with benthic sampling enabled the multibeam data to be used to classify and map the seabed.\u003c/gmd:LocalisedCharacterString\u003e           \u003c/gmd:textGroup\u003e         \u003c/gmd:PT_FreeText\u003e       \u003c/gmd:abstract\u003e       \u003cgmd:pointOfContact\u003e         \u003cgmd:CI_ResponsibleParty\u003e           \u003c